+\subsection{Curvature on analytic components}
+\label{sec:ch14-component-curvature}
+
+Equality of canonical curvature need not be separated component by component
+by an algebraic cancellation of currents at their intersections.  The
+following consequence of the analytic-nullity theorem
+\cite[Corollary~16.9.4]{CLD25} supplies a sufficient condition.  It concerns
+curvatures of specified potentials, not arbitrary positive currents.
+
+We use two classes of functions.  Write
+$\operatorname{CPsh}_Y$ for the least subsheaf of the real continuous
+functions on $Y$ containing the ordinary CLD-smooth psh functions and stable
+under locally uniform limits.  This is
+\cite[Definition~8.1.7 and Lemma~8.1.8]{CLD25}, developed further in
+\Cref{sec:ch16-hypotheses,lem:ch16-cpsh-induction}.
+Write $\mathscr A(Y)$ for the real continuous functions which, on one
+ordinary neighbourhood of every point, are uniform limits of a sequence of
+ordinary smooth psh functions defined on that same neighbourhood.
+Compact-uniform convergence gives the same local condition after a
+relatively compact shrinking.  Unrelated shrinking domains for the terms
+of a sequence do not satisfy this definition.
+
+A member of $\mathscr A(Y)$ is locally approachable in the first-stage
+sense of \cite[Definition~8.1.13]{CLD25}.  Indeed, after shrinking, let
+$f_j\to f$ uniformly and put $e_j=\|f_j-f\|_\infty$.  If infinitely many
+$e_j$ vanish, $f$ is smooth psh.  Otherwise discard the zero-error terms
+and choose a subsequence with $e_{j+1}<e_j/3$.  Then
+\[
+ f_{j+1}+2e_{j+1}
+ \leq f+3e_{j+1}
+ \leq f+e_j
+ \leq f_j+2e_j,
+\]
+so these smooth psh functions decrease uniformly to $f$.  In particular
+$\mathscr A$ is contained in $\operatorname{CPsh}$.  We do not identify it
+with the full uniform-closure sheaf.
+
+\begin{proposition}
+\label{prop:ch14-component-curvature}
+Let $K$ be any complete non-Archimedean field, possibly trivially valued.
+Let $X$ be good, separated, usual-strictly $K$-analytic, boundaryless and
+pure of dimension $n\geq1$.  Let $(X_i)_i$ be its reduced analytic
+irreducible components, let $j_i:X_i\hookrightarrow X$, and put
+$\delta_i=(j_i)_*\delta_{X_i}$.  Write $m_i>0$ for the generic
+multiplicities of $X$, and set
+\[
+ B_i=X_i\cap\bigcup_{j\ne i}X_j,
+ \qquad B=\bigcup_iB_i.
+\]
+The components and these unions are understood over the original field
+$K$.  Then the following assertions hold.
+\begin{enumerate}
+\item\label{itm:ch14-component-curvature:approachable}
+For $u,v\in\mathscr A(X)$,
+\begin{equation}
+ \dprimeddprime(u\delta_X)=\dprimeddprime(v\delta_X)
+ \quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad
+ \dprimeddprime(u\delta_i)=\dprimeddprime(v\delta_i)
+ \ \text{for every }i .
+ \label{eq:ch14-component-curvature-equivalence}
+\end{equation}
+\item\label{itm:ch14-component-curvature:common}
+Let $u,v\in\operatorname{CPsh}_X(X)$ have common canonical curvature
+$S=\dprimeddprime[u]=\dprimeddprime[v]$, where $[w]=w\delta_X$.
+Suppose that, for every compactly supported positive smooth
+$(n-1,n-1)$-form $\alpha$, the positive Radon measure $S\wedge\alpha$
+gives zero mass to $B$.  Then
+\begin{equation}
+                 \dprimeddprime(u\delta_i)
+                    =\dprimeddprime(v\delta_i)
+                    \quad\text{for every }i .
+ \label{eq:ch14-component-common-curvature}
+\end{equation}
+The zero-mass hypothesis is equivalent to total-variation nullity on $B$
+for every signed coefficient measure $S\wedge\alpha$.  It is satisfied
+in each of the following cases:
+\begin{enumerate}
+\item\label{itm:ch14-component-curvature:one-side}
+one of $u,v$ belongs locally to $\mathscr A$;
+\item\label{itm:ch14-component-curvature:local-potential}
+every point has an ordinary neighbourhood on which $S=\dprimeddprime[a]$
+for some $a\in\mathscr A$;
+\item\label{itm:ch14-component-curvature:zero}
+$S=0$.
+\end{enumerate}
+\end{enumerate}
+No normality, reducedness, perfectness, discreteness or
+residue-characteristic restriction is imposed.  Component intersections
+may have dimension $n-1$.
+\end{proposition}
+
+\begin{proof}
+\emph{Component geometry and canonical normalization.}
+The component family is G-locally finite by
+\cite[Theorem~4.20]{Ducros09Excellence}; in particular, an affinoid domain
+meets only finitely many components.  A compact set in the good space $X$
+is covered by finitely many affinoid neighbourhoods, so only finitely many
+components occur near any compact test support.  The closed-union and
+dimension statements in
+\cite[\S4, especially Theorem~4.20 and paragraphs~4.21--4.24]{Ducros09Excellence}
+show that $B_i$ is closed analytic in $X$ and in $X_i$, of local dimension
+less than $n$.  The locally finite union $B$ has the same properties.
+These are analytic components over $K$, not geometric components after
+extension of the field.
+
+Each $j_i$ is a finite closed immersion and has no relative boundary.
+Thus $X_i$ is again good, separated, usual-strict and boundaryless, of pure
+dimension $n$.  Its integration current and its proper pushforward are
+positive and separately closed by Stokes and
+\Cref{prop:current-cycle-closed,prop:current-pushforward-formal}.
+The fundamental-cycle identity
+\Cref{thm:current-fundamental-cycle} gives the locally finite equality
+\begin{equation}
+                         \delta_X=\sum_i m_i\delta_i.
+ \label{eq:ch14-component-canonical-sum}
+\end{equation}
+The positive generic lengths $m_i$ are retained also when $X$ is
+nonreduced.
+
+\emph{Positive curvature and the first-stage nullity input.}
+For a positive separately closed current $Q$ and
+$w\in\operatorname{CPsh}$, the current
+$\dprimeddprime(wQ)$ is positive and separately closed by
+\cite[Lemma~8.5.5]{CLD25}.  The full uniform-closure scope is important:
+the continuous functions $w$ for which this current is positive form a
+subsheaf, contain the smooth psh functions, and are stable under locally
+uniform limits.  Continuous multiplication of the fixed positive current
+and differentiation are continuous, and the positive cone is closed.
+Minimality of $\operatorname{CPsh}$ proves the assertion without producing
+one smooth sequence for a general $w$.
+
+For $w\in\mathscr A(X)$ put $S_i^w=\dprimeddprime(w\delta_i)$.
+Pullback by $j_i$ preserves smooth psh functions and their uniform
+approximations on an ordinary neighbourhood.  Only functions and smooth
+forms are pulled back.  Proper pushforward, continuous multiplication and
+differentiation therefore give
+\begin{equation}
+ S_i^w
+    =(j_i)_*\dprimeddprime[w|_{X_i}],
+ \qquad
+ S_i^w\wedge\alpha
+    =(j_i)_*\bigl(\dprimeddprime[w|_{X_i}]
+                              \wedge j_i^*\alpha\bigr).
+ \label{eq:ch14-component-coefficient-pushforward}
+\end{equation}
+The first equality is also \cite[Proposition~8.5.6]{CLD25}.
+For the second, $j_i^*\alpha$ has compact support when $\alpha$ does,
+since $j_i$ is topologically proper.  Testing against compactly supported
+smooth scalar functions proves the displayed identity; density of those
+tests in continuous compact tests identifies the corresponding Radon
+measures.  This is not pullback of an arbitrary current through a singular
+map.
+
+Apply \cite[Corollary~16.9.4]{CLD25}, with one locally approachable
+continuous potential, on $X_i$.  It gives zero mass on $B_i$ for every
+positive coefficient measure on the right of
+\eqref{eq:ch14-component-coefficient-pushforward}.  We use precisely this
+published first-stage analytic-nullity input, not an extension to every
+member of $\operatorname{CPsh}$.  For a general smooth test $\alpha$,
+discard its antisymmetric part, which pairs to zero with a symmetric
+curvature current, and locally write its symmetric part as a difference
+of two positive compact smooth forms.  The total variation of the
+difference of the two resulting measures is bounded by their sum.
+Thus source nullity is total-variation nullity, not merely signed
+cancellation.  Proper pushforward of a signed measure satisfies
+$|(j_i)_*\mu|\leq(j_i)_*|\mu|$, and $j_i^{-1}(B_i)$ is the same crossing
+subset on $X_i$.  Consequently
+\begin{equation}
+                         |S_i^w\wedge\alpha|(B_i)=0
+                         \quad(w\in\mathscr A(X)).
+ \label{eq:ch14-component-curvature-nullity}
+\end{equation}
+Compact smooth partitions and cutoffs justify each localization.
+No global paracompactness or global finite-component assumption is needed.
+
+\emph{The equivalence for two first-stage potentials.}
+Set $R_i=S_i^u-S_i^v$.  Differentiating
+\eqref{eq:ch14-component-canonical-sum} gives
+\begin{equation}
+ \dprimeddprime(u\delta_X)-\dprimeddprime(v\delta_X)
+                              =\sum_i m_i R_i .
+ \label{eq:ch14-component-curvature-difference}
+\end{equation}
+This proves the reverse implication of
+\eqref{eq:ch14-component-curvature-equivalence}.
+For the forward implication, assume the left side of
+\eqref{eq:ch14-component-curvature-difference} is zero.
+The current $R_i$ vanishes outside $X_i$ by support.  On the ordinary
+open $X\setminus\bigcup_{j\ne i}X_j$ the canonical current is
+$m_i\delta_i$, so $m_iR_i=0$ there.  Since $m_i>0$, these two ordinary
+open sets give
+\begin{equation}
+                              \Supp R_i\subseteq B_i .
+ \label{eq:ch14-component-curvature-support}
+\end{equation}
+No division of a current identity at a shared point is involved.
+
+For any compact smooth $(n-1,n-1)$-test $\alpha$, the signed measure
+$R_i\wedge\alpha$ vanishes on $X\setminus B_i$, by
+\eqref{eq:ch14-component-curvature-support} and smooth-test density.
+Its total variation on $B_i$ is zero by applying
+\eqref{eq:ch14-component-curvature-nullity} separately to $u$ and $v$.
+It is therefore the zero measure.  A compact smooth scalar cutoff equal
+to one near $\Supp\alpha$ recovers
+$\langle R_i,\alpha\rangle=0$.  All compact tests have been accounted for,
+so $R_i=0$, proving
+\cref{itm:ch14-component-curvature:approachable}.
+
+\emph{The common-curvature criterion.}
+For arbitrary $u,v\in\operatorname{CPsh}_X(X)$ define
+$S_i^u=\dprimeddprime(u\delta_i)$ and
+$S_i^v=\dprimeddprime(v\delta_i)$ directly on $X$.  They are positive by
+the full-sheaf positivity argument above.  Their common canonical
+curvature has the two positive decompositions
+\begin{equation}
+                 S=\sum_jm_jS_j^u=\sum_jm_jS_j^v .
+ \label{eq:ch14-component-common-sums}
+\end{equation}
+For every compact positive $\alpha$, these are inequalities of positive
+Radon measures:
+\begin{equation}
+ 0\leq S_i^u\wedge\alpha\leq m_i^{-1}(S\wedge\alpha),
+ \qquad
+ 0\leq S_i^v\wedge\alpha\leq m_i^{-1}(S\wedge\alpha).
+ \label{eq:ch14-component-common-domination}
+\end{equation}
+Indeed, removing one summand from either locally finite sum in
+\eqref{eq:ch14-component-common-sums} leaves a positive current.
+Nonnegative smooth scalar tests and then their density among continuous
+compact tests give \eqref{eq:ch14-component-common-domination} as measure
+inequalities.  The assumed nullity on $B$ therefore gives nullity on
+$B_i$ for both component measures.  Decomposition into positive forms
+gives total-variation nullity for all their signed coefficients.
+
+The support argument
+\eqref{eq:ch14-component-curvature-support} used only continuous
+multipliers and equality of canonical curvature, so it still applies.
+Combining it with the just proved nullity proves $R_i=0$ exactly as
+before, and establishes
+\cref{itm:ch14-component-curvature:common}.
+The same decomposition of tests proves the stated equivalence between
+positive-coefficient nullity and signed total-variation nullity.
+
+For \cref{itm:ch14-component-curvature:one-side}, apply the first-stage
+theorem \cite[Corollary~16.9.4]{CLD25} to the one approachable potential
+representing $S$ on $X$.  For
+\cref{itm:ch14-component-curvature:local-potential}, apply it on each
+ordinary neighbourhood with potential $a$; a finite smooth partition
+near each compact test support gives nullity on all of $B$.  The source
+uses canonical integration also on nonreduced spaces.  Equivalently,
+apply it on the reduced components and retain the positive
+multiplicities in \eqref{eq:ch14-component-canonical-sum}.
+Finally, in \cref{itm:ch14-component-curvature:zero},
+\eqref{eq:ch14-component-common-sums} is a positive weighted sum equal
+to zero, so every component curvature already vanishes.
+\end{proof}
+
+All neighbourhoods and tests in this argument are ordinary analytic
+ones.  If the proposition is applied to an ordinary open satisfying its
+ambient hypotheses, its components are the components of that open,
+not presumed to be intersections with a fixed global component family.
+In particular no inheritance of usual strictness by every ordinary open
+over a trivially valued field is asserted.
+Nor does \Cref{prop:ch14-component-curvature} classify arbitrary positive
+closed currents or assert analytic-nullity for an arbitrary common
+$\operatorname{CPsh}$ curvature.  The first-stage condition, or the
+explicit common-curvature nullity condition, remains part of the result.
+
+
